\begin{abstract}
Schema complexity affects training-free LLM information extraction through two independent dimensions: type count drives recall decline, while inter-type confusability drives type substitution errors. We present what is, to our knowledge, the first controlled study isolating these factors in zero-shot LLM IE across NER, RE, and event detection on four datasets. Using embedding-based confusability metrics (SCS/BCS) with controlled batch design, we find:
(1)~count degradation spans all three tasks (up to $-$49\,pp); confusability-driven substitution is documented primarily in RE, with bounded interaction;
(2)~BCS explains ${\sim}$22\% of cross-batch RE accuracy variance on FewRel (ecological level), replicating across three open-weight model families (8B--70B); NER batch-level signal is not detected at current power;
(3)~schema decomposition fails due to merge amplification and routing failure, though oracle routing confirms the two dimensions are independently addressable.
\end{abstract}

